  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1246 PM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe pattern across the CONUS starts with a trough across the east \nand ridge in the southwest that waves are moving eastward overtop \nof. One such trough vacated the area this morning bringing \nsubsidence into Monday. A brief period of warm advection later \nMonday into Monday night is overtaken by a brief period cold \nadvection as another wave passes by to our north in the northwest \nflow. Not much surface cooling occurs as the cold front washes out \nacross the area and it's a dry frontal passage. \n\nThis series of waves has been able to affect the ridge in the west \nso that by the time the next wave overtops it, it's able to dive \nfarther southward. At the same time, an upper low in eastern Canada \nwill help to push some cold air in behind this wave so that it'll \nlikely be rain at the start here later Wednesday. Then, we'll have \nto watch for the timing of the cold air arrival and if there's going \nto be some low level moisture to tap into. Lake effect precipitation \ncould change to snow Thursday night into Friday as the cold air \nmakes it to the surface. 850 mb temperatures get to -4 to -8C \nThursday night, which is right around what's needed to produce lake \neffect snow. Trajectories will also matter for where the snow bands \nend up. That will be out of the area by Sunday night or Monday as \nsurface high pressure noses in. Those 850 mb temps don't rise above \nfreezing until we get to next week. GFS soundings are normally dry \nthat far out so it'll be interesting to see how the short range \nguidance handles it and this will help to address whether snow is \nheavy enough to overcome antecedant warm air. The NBM has about a 20-\n30 percent chance of measureable (>0.1\") of snow late Thu night-Fri \nAM. The NBM has high temperatures rebounding back into the 40s at \nleast by the weekend and potentially as early as Friday meaning the \nsnow should disappear quickly especially if that dry air remains in \natmospheric column. Wind gusts also increase to 25 to 40 mph on \nThursday with 25 to 30 mph gusts still possible Friday and Saturday.\n\nDry air takes over into the first half of next week after the \ncool/wetter air moves eastward as mid level ridging/surface high \npressure slide southeastward.",
